{"machine": "D10 HMS Audacity", "source_fingerprint": "f9872ce7d5f931f3276b1060f906c6a952ddce8e5bf8d8653e46a9b4c42644a1", "result": {"paragraph": "HMS Audacity (D10) was the first Royal Navy auxiliary aircraft carrier, converted from the captured German merchant ship Hannover, which was intercepted in the West Indies in March 1940. The Admiralty decided that small carriers were part of the solution for convoy defence, and Empire Audacity was among the merchantmen converted. She was commissioned for service in the Western Approaches for convoy defence. The ship was a flush deck carrier with no island structure or hangar deck. Her Martlet fighters shot down two Condors and contributed to the scuttling of U-131.", "word_count": 91, "passed": true, "warnings": [], "claim_map": [{"sentence": "HMS Audacity (D10) was the first Royal Navy auxiliary aircraft carrier, converted from the captured German merchant ship Hannover, which was intercepted in the West Indies in March 1940.", "citations": [{"excerpt_id": "S3-E1", "source_id": "S3", "source_title": "HMS Audacity, British escort carrier, WW2", "source_url": "https://naval-history.net/xGM-Chrono-05CVE-Audacity.htm", "locator": "S3-E1; query=\"D10 HMS Audacity\" official history commissioned", "quote": "HMS Audacity, British escort carrier, WW2 Naval History Homepage and Site Search SERVICE HISTORIES of ROYAL NAVY WARSHIPS in WORLD WAR 2 by Lt Cdr Geoffrey B Mason RN (Rtd) (c) 2006 HMS AUDACITY (D 10), first escort carrier conversion Edited by Gordon Smith, Naval-History.Net HMS Audacity ( CyberHeritage, no enlargement) return to Contents List First Royal Navy Auxiliary Aircraft Carrier which, as German mercantile HANNOVER, was captured on 8th March 1940 when intercepted in the West Indies by HM Cruiser DUNEDIN and HM\ufffd Canadian Destroyer ASSINIBOINE. She had been laid down for use as a Refrigerated Cargo Ship and had completed build at Bremen in May 1939.", "source_capture_method": "fetched_page"}, {"excerpt_id": "S1-E6", "source_id": "S1", "source_title": "HMS Audacity - Wikipedia", "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HMS_Audacity", "locator": "S1-E6; query=\"D10 HMS Audacity\" official history commissioned", "quote": "She was originally the German merchant ship Hannover , which the British captured in the West Indies in March 1940 and renamed Sinbad , then Empire Audacity .", "source_capture_method": "fetched_page"}]}, {"sentence": "The Admiralty decided that small carriers were part of the solution for convoy defence, and Empire Audacity was among the merchantmen converted.", "citations": [{"excerpt_id": "S1-E8", "source_id": "S1", "source_title": "HMS Audacity - Wikipedia", "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HMS_Audacity", "locator": "S1-E8; query=\"D10 HMS Audacity\" official history commissioned", "quote": "The Admiralty decided that small carriers were part of the solution and had a number of merchantmen, including Empire Audacity , converted.", "source_capture_method": "fetched_page"}]}, {"sentence": "She was commissioned for service in the Western Approaches for convoy defence.", "citations": [{"excerpt_id": "S3-E5", "source_id": "S3", "source_title": "HMS Audacity, British escort carrier, WW2", "source_url": "https://naval-history.net/xGM-Chrono-05CVE-Audacity.htm", "locator": "S3-E5; query=\"D10 HMS Audacity\" official history commissioned", "quote": "\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd 20th\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd Commissioned for service as HMS EMPIRE AUDACITY for service in Western \ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd\ufffd Approaches for convoy defence.", "source_capture_method": "fetched_page"}]}, {"sentence": "The ship was a flush deck carrier with no island structure or hangar deck.", "citations": [{"excerpt_id": "S4-E8", "source